# The Sun-Cake (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md; NPC plan batch 3): the Baron wants a cake shaped like the sun for
# the festival, and his cook Leontina, who has made four hundred and thirteen round things, wants to make one that
# isn't. She needs eggs (Goodwife Marta), honey (Old Paraschiva at the Vistani camp) and saffron (Vadoma); each can be
# bought outright or haggled, then the party helps bake it. Ratatouille takes over if he's along; otherwise a steady
# hand or a good eye; a collapsed cake the Baron declares a triumph anyway. No fight, and nothing that a plain option
# doesn't reach (a gold piece buys each ingredient). Lines drafted by Gemini through the ai-bridge, edited; unvoiced.
# It can fail (sq-stuck, Ashwin's rule that side quests can be lost): once the Tser Pool camp is broken Vadoma will not sell
# the saffron (vadoma:start jumps to vadoma_no_saffron), and with no saffron there is no sun: the quest closes as failed and
# Leontina says so (leontina_failed). Eggs and honey have no such end: Marta sells after the festival too, and Paraschiva
# is a different Vistana at a different camp. Whoever rules the mansion tastes the cake (taste_*).

# --- Leontina in the burgomaster's mansion (npc leontina) ---

~ leontina
if quest.the_sun_cake == done
-> leontina_after
endif
if quest.the_sun_cake == failed
-> leontina_failed
endif
if not quest.the_sun_cake
quest the_sun_cake asked
Narrator: Leontina stands at the long table in a spotless apron, surrounded by a hundred identical round pastries.
Leontina: Don't touch the trays. Wipe your boots if you're staying, and speak up if you aren't.
Leontina [weary]: The Baron wants a great sun cake for the festival. I have baked four hundred and thirteen suns in this house, every one of them round.
Leontina: A disk is not a sun. A plate is round. A coin is round. If I roll out one more circle, I'll put my head in the oven.
Leontina: This one will have proper rays and corners, or I'm done. Only the pantry is bare of what makes a crust hold its shape.
Leontina: I need fresh eggs from Goodwife Marta, dark honey from Old Paraschiva at the camp, and saffron from Vadoma's wagon. I cannot leave the stove.
Leontina: Mind your manners at the camp: Vadoma only opens her boxes for people the Vistani still speak to.
-> leontina_menu
endif
if flag.cake_eggs and flag.cake_honey and flag.cake_saffron
-> bake_start
endif
Leontina: I need fresh eggs from Goodwife Marta, dark honey from Old Paraschiva at the camp, and saffron from Vadoma's wagon. I cannot leave the stove.
-> leontina_menu

~ leontina_menu
* You sound like you have cooked somewhere grand. -> leontina_secret
* Goodbye. -> END

~ leontina_secret
Leontina: You're staring at my hands. Yes, I know how to hold a paring blade. I didn't learn that boiling turnips in Barovia.
Leontina [sad]: Before the mists took the road, I was third pastry cook in a great kitchen where the copper pots shone like mirrors.
Leontina: The mistress there could make puff pastry sing in the pan. Nobody in Vallaki believes a word of it, of course.
Leontina: See this white line across my thumb? A blade that could split a hair cleanly in two. You don't get that scar from a dull bread knife.
-> leontina_menu

~ leontina_after
if flag.cake_result == "good"
Leontina [smile]: Take another look at my thumb before you go. That white line is the only proof I have left that I wasn't born in the dark.
Leontina: Today, for the first time in twenty years, I think the old mistress would have recognized my work.
else
Leontina: Next week I will hammer the iron into a simple square and use one less egg yolk in the wash.
Leontina: Don't look so mournful. A cake that collapses in the pan has still been baked, and at least it wasn't round.
endif
-> END

~ leontina_failed
Leontina [weary]: No saffron, then. Well, you can't bake a sun with corners out of pale dough and good intentions.
Leontina: I rolled the thing out with a wine bottle at dawn, four hundred and fourteen, as round and dull as a kettle lid.
Leontina: You tried, I suppose, which is more than the rest of this house does, though trying doesn't put a crust on a cake.
Leontina: There's no coin for an empty hand, so take the smell of the kitchen with you and shut the door against the damp.
-> END

# --- The three ingredients (townsfolk:goodwife, camp_folk:cook, vadoma:menu jump here) ---

~ marta_eggs
Goodwife Marta [smile]: Eggs for the mansion? Of course! Wonderful! It is a blessing to feed the Baron's household.
Goodwife Marta: One gold piece for the dozen, brown and speckled, laid this very morning before the fog lifted.
Goodwife Marta [weary]: She'll be making another of those blessed yellow circles, I suppose. Round tarts, round loaves, round biscuits.
Goodwife Marta: I pray every night that someone in this town bakes a square, just once, before I die.
-> marta_menu

~ marta_menu
* [if gold >= 1] (Pay the gold piece.) -> marta_pay
* [Persuasion DC 11] (Ask for them for the Baron's cook.) -> marta_free | marta_menu
* Another time. -> END

~ marta_pay
gold -1
set cake_eggs
Goodwife Marta [smile]: Twelve speckled ones, all whole, wrapped in my clean linen so they don't knock together on the cobbles.
Goodwife Marta [afraid]: Carry them like you're carrying the Baron's temper, dear: one crack on the way up the hill and we all hear about it.
-> END

~ marta_free
set cake_eggs
Goodwife Marta [sly]: You drive a hard bargain, and if it's truly to help Leontina break the mould, keep your coin.
Goodwife Marta: Take the basket for nothing. For the Baron's cook, and for a crust with proper corners, my hens are happy to oblige.
-> END

~ paraschiva_honey
Old Paraschiva: For the burgomaster's kitchen, is it? Tell him my bees don't care about his decrees or his wicker suns.
Old Paraschiva [weary]: Still, Leontina knows what to do with a comb, which is more than I can say for the rest of that town.
Old Paraschiva: Two gold pieces for the clay pot. Mind the string, I've wrapped the stopper in a fresh dock leaf to keep the damp out.
Old Paraschiva: Don't spill a drop on the road. That's the very last of last year's heather, and this summer gave us nothing but rain.
-> paraschiva_menu

~ paraschiva_menu
* [if gold >= 2] (Pay the two gold pieces.) -> paraschiva_pay
* [Persuasion DC 11] (Ask for the pot for the Baron's cook.) -> paraschiva_free | paraschiva_menu
* Another time. -> END

~ paraschiva_pay
gold -2
set cake_honey
Old Paraschiva: Two gold is fair. Keep the cord knotted around the stopper, or the damp gets in and turns the heather sour.
Old Paraschiva [weary]: And keep to the middle of the road going back. What waits in the ditch has no teeth for honey, only for the hand holding it.
-> END

~ paraschiva_free
set cake_honey
Old Paraschiva [sly]: Hmph. You have a Vistani tongue in a stranger's mouth. Keep your gold, then.
Old Paraschiva: Sing me something decent at the fire next time you pass and we are square.
-> END

~ vadoma_saffron
Vadoma [sly]: Saffron, in this valley? You might as well ask me to bottle the morning star for your kitchen.
Vadoma: It is dearer than gold here, friend, where nothing yellow grows unless the Baron paints it himself.
Vadoma: I have one pinch left, taken from the lining of a dead gentleman's velvet coat. Five gold pieces, and not a copper less.
Narrator: She opens a painted cedar box and lifts a tiny twist of crimson silk between two ringed fingers.
-> vadoma_menu

# Vadoma's refusal once the camp at the Tser Pool is broken (vadoma:start jumps here while the cake is asked and unfinished):
# there is no other saffron in the valley, so the sun is lost.
~ vadoma_no_saffron
quest the_sun_cake failed
Narrator: A cold draught smells of woodsmoke and wet wool as Vadoma rests her ringed hand flat upon the shut lid of the painted cedar box.
Vadoma [angry]: Keep your coin in your purse, and tell the Baron's cook her oven can go cold for all I care.
Vadoma: Blood was spilled by the Tser Pool, and there is not enough gold in this valley to buy one thread of crimson from me.
-> END

~ vadoma_menu
* [if gold >= 5] (Pay the five gold pieces.) -> vadoma_pay
* [if gold >= 3] [Persuasion DC 12] (Haggle.) -> vadoma_cheap | vadoma_menu
* Another time. -> END

~ vadoma_pay
gold -5
set cake_saffron
Vadoma [sly]: Into the little painted box it goes, silk and all, so the damp doesn't steal the colour before your cook does.
Vadoma: A dead gentleman leaves behind boots that pinch, a ring that won't come off, and just enough spice to flavour someone else's soup.
-> END

~ vadoma_cheap
gold -3
set cake_saffron
Vadoma [weary]: Three gold, then, and take it before I remember how far I rode to find that coat.
Vadoma [sly]: Looking at the silk again, the pinch is a little smaller than I thought anyway.
-> END

# --- Baking it ---

~ bake_start
quest the_sun_cake ready
Narrator: The mansion kitchen smells of woodsmoke and warm butter. Your eggs, honey and saffron sit lined up on the scrubbed oak board.
Narrator: Behind Leontina, the great brick oven glows a sullen, steady red.
Narrator: On the shelf sits the battered round tin of four hundred festivals. Beside it rests an iron frame hammered into eight sharp, stubborn points.
Leontina: Everything is measured. The butter is cold, the honey is warm, and the saffron is steeping in cream.
Leontina: Pick up the rolling pin. It is time we made something in this house that has an edge to it.
* [name:ratatoille] (Let Ratatouille take over the dough.) -> bake_ratatouille
* [Dexterity DC 12] (Shape the cake with a steady hand.) -> bake_ok | bake_flat
* [Wisdom DC 12] (Judge the oven.) -> bake_ok | bake_flat
* (Leave it to Leontina.) -> bake_flat

~ bake_ratatouille
set cake_result = "good"
interject name:ratatoille: Stand back, please, everyone. The ambient humidity in this kitchen is appalling, and the dough requires a delicate hand.
Narrator: Ratatouille takes the board with the fussy, serene confidence of a man who has waited years for someone to hand him an apron.
Leontina: Look at the way he turns the wrist on the fold. Stop talking, all of you; he trained somewhere real.
Narrator: Through the iron grating of the oven door, the saffron crust rises evenly into eight crisp, golden points.
-> verdict_good

~ bake_ok
set cake_result = "good"
Narrator: You press the chilled pastry into the iron angles with a steady thumb, watching the heat of your hands and the draught from the door.
Narrator: You judge the embers by their ash and pull the tin three breaths before the honey can catch.
Narrator: It turns out onto the platter a little lopsided, bright with saffron, and unmistakably, gloriously eight-pointed.
Leontina [sad]: Look at the points on that crust. Excuse me a moment, the woodsmoke has gotten into my eyes.
-> verdict_good

~ bake_flat
set cake_result = "flat"
Narrator: A draught catches the oven door, or perhaps the honey was too heavy for the flour. A dull sigh comes from inside the bricks.
Narrator: When you draw the iron frame out, the points have surrendered into a wide, bubbling golden puddle.
Narrator: Down the hall, the Baron's herald is already blowing his brass horn to announce the festival centerpiece.
Narrator: Leontina stands in the kitchen doorway with her floury arms folded tight across her apron, and says nothing at all for a very long time.
-> verdict_flat

~ verdict_good
quest the_sun_cake done
if flag.vallaki_backed == "wachter"
-> taste_wachter_good
elif flag.vallaki_backed == "neither"
-> taste_deposed_good
endif
Narrator: The Baron takes a forkful before the assembled household, chewing while his guards watch his eyebrows.
Baron [smile]: Magnificent! Look at the points on it! Like rays of glory bursting through the abominable fog!
Baron: A triumph, I say! A masterpiece! A culinary scandal in the very finest sense of the word!
Baron: From this day forward, Leontina, you have my full permission to abandon the circle whenever inspiration strikes!
Narrator: Behind his shoulder, Leontina catches your eye and gives a single, brisk nod.
-> verdict_good_pay

# Lady Wachter rules the mansion: she tastes it (sq-stuck, finding 124).
~ taste_wachter_good
Narrator: Lady Wachter lifts a crisp, saffron-bright point to her lips, the warm scent of dark honey rising above her teacup.
Lady Wachter [smile]: Eight sharp corners, and every one of them crisp: my friends, Leontina has outdone herself.
Lady Wachter: For years Vargas fed this house little round loaves like wet millstones and called it sunshine.
Lady Wachter: We have no need of festivals now, of course, though we shall certainly finish the cake before curfew.
Narrator: Behind Lady Wachter's chair, Leontina catches your eye and gives a single, brisk nod.
-> verdict_good_pay

# The council has deposed the Baron, who still lives in the mansion.
~ taste_deposed_good
Narrator: In his shirtsleeves, the Baron breaks off a warm, saffron-bright point, getting crumbs upon his open festival ledger.
Baron [smile]: Magnificent! Look at the points on it, sharp as morning bursting through the abominable fog!
Baron [sad]: Four hundred and thirteen round suns this house has baked me, and here is the first with corners, now there is no square to show it to.
Baron: Still, we shall eat it here, and someone ought to tell Father Lucian that the corners held.
Narrator: Behind his shoulder, Leontina catches your eye and gives a single, brisk nod.
-> verdict_good_pay

~ verdict_good_pay
gold +60
inspire party: baked the Baron a sun that wasn't round
approve ratatoille +2 liriel_dawnsong +1 kip_smudgewick +1: You baked the Baron's sun-cake
Leontina: Here is what I promised from the housekeeping purse, and a warm corner of the crust wrapped in linen for the road.
Leontina: You have steady hands and you know when to leave an oven door shut. That is rarer than saffron.
Leontina [smile]: Take another look at my thumb before you go. That white line is the only proof I have left that I wasn't born in the dark.
Leontina: Today, for the first time in twenty years, I think the old mistress would have recognized my work.
-> END

~ verdict_flat
quest the_sun_cake done
if flag.vallaki_backed == "wachter"
-> taste_wachter_flat
elif flag.vallaki_backed == "neither"
-> taste_deposed_flat
endif
Narrator: The Baron spoons up a warm drip of the golden puddle while the household holds its breath.
Baron [smile]: Extraordinary! It has melted from the sheer, uncontainable heat of its own joy! A triumph!
Baron: Why, it is the most innovative piece of confectionery in the history of Vallaki! A sun upon the waters!
Narrator: Leontina's face does not move by so much as the breadth of a hair.
Baron [smile]: We shall call it the Liquid Dawn! Leontina, my treasure, you must bake four hundred and fourteen of these before winter!
-> verdict_flat_pay

~ taste_wachter_flat
Narrator: Lady Wachter touches a silver fork to the wide, bubbling golden puddle and tastes a single drop of the hot syrup.
Lady Wachter [smile]: How very intriguing, my friends: a sun that has chosen to lie down flat upon the platter.
Lady Wachter: The saffron is quite unmistakable, and one hardly misses the corners once one accepts the shape of it.
Lady Wachter: Do take a spoon, everyone, before it cools to the dish; we must not let Leontina's trouble go to waste.
Narrator: By the sideboard, Leontina's face does not move by so much as the breadth of a hair.
-> verdict_flat_pay

~ taste_deposed_flat
Narrator: The Baron dips a finger into the wide, bubbling golden puddle, the warm scent of honey rising over his open ledger.
Baron [smile]: A triumph, I say, a glorious golden sea of a cake, and the colour of it is quite beyond reproach!
Baron [sad]: The points have melted away, perhaps, though nobody in the square will be waiting to count them today.
Baron: Someone ought to write it down in the ledger all the same, just so the house remembers we had saffron once.
Narrator: Behind his shoulder, Leontina's face does not move by so much as the breadth of a hair.
-> verdict_flat_pay

~ verdict_flat_pay
gold +20
Leontina [weary]: Here is your coin. It's what I can spare from the kitchen tallow fund, and you did fetch what I asked.
Leontina: Next week I will hammer the iron into a simple square and use one less egg yolk in the wash.
Leontina: Don't look so mournful. A cake that collapses in the pan has still been baked, and at least it wasn't round.
-> END
